      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14246155/assets/d04a8e993da8e90ca75f5f7541317d26a6a9c8f9b76cdb9c95511080035177c5.png)\n\nFind the cosine of $∠Y.$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall the definition of cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡Y=\\frac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}=\\frac{XY}{YZ}=\\frac{XY}{5}\n$$\n\nHere, we need to determine the missing side $XY.$ The trick is to use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{Y{Z}^{2}-X{Z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}-(\\sqrt{7})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25-7} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡Y & =\\frac{XY}{5}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
