      "dom_id": "question-122050",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "In the right triangle $△JKL,$ $∠J$ is a right angle, $JL=8$ and $KL=12.$ Find $\\operatorname{tan}⁡K.$",
      "worked_solution": "The following triangle illustrates the information given.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14246155/assets/cc33327cc6d071cb6ec9ac0c10d58e57bc35f8d81b326731120cf04c18f58ec1.png)\n\nRecall the definition of tangent: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡K=\\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{adjacent}}=\\frac{JL}{JK}=\\frac{8}{JK}\n$$\n\nHere, we need to determine the missing side $JK.$ The trick is to use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}JK & =\\sqrt{K{L}^{2}-J{L}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{12}^{2}-{8}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{144-64} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{80} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡K & =\\frac{8}{4\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
